186045417] [17592186106760 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106761 #edb/ref 1061 27300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106761 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec55bd0b-4d12-5185-b2f3-c2f5b6e10834" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106761 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106762 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106762 #edb/ref 1061 27301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106762 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c81f0294-20be-52d5-b339-bc0866cf7bdc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106762 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106763 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106763 #edb/ref 1061 27302 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106763 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8be7abf-649f-5403-aa2f-6829e8676c16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106763 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106793 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106793 #edb/ref 1061 28351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106793 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0cfde5a4-e03f-5b7b-b7a3-084b4e188e40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106793 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106831 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106831 #edb/ref 1061 38089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106831 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ee2b1f28-9218-55e7-85d6-9d0eb6abb586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106831 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106832 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106832 #edb/ref 1061 38090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106832 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3e5fcbe4-37c4-5421-b3a8-e040c80da52d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106832 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107065 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ below. How can we determine the intervals where $f'(x)$ is positive or negative?\n\nThe first step is to identify the points where the derivative is zero. Here, the critical points are $x=2$ (a local maximum) and $x=4$ (a local minimum), and $f'(x) = 0$ at these points.\n\n![](images/de/de01a8dec747f86d6b276431ef02675d3827028508be9aa9ca3699ca4e6bcd6c.png)\n\nThe next step is to identify where the function is increasing and decreasing. Below, the intervals where $f(x)$ is increasing are shown in blue, and the intervals where $f(x)$ is decreasing are shown in green.\n\n![](images/10/10edc10cefa3cc48713795e0e2f40b05ba6174e2da7aa5698a02fc35444b1c45.png)\n\nSince we know where the function is increasing or decreasing, we can determine where $f'(x)$ is positive or negative.\n\n- When $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, the slopes of the tangent lines are positive, so we have \n\n$$\nf'(x)>0.\n$$\n\n- When $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the slopes of the tangent lines are negative, so we have \n\n$$\nf'(x)<0.\n$$\n\nThe information about $f'(x)$ can be  summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,2) & 2 & (2,4) & 4 & (4,+\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ \\\\ {f}^{'} & + & 0 & - & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, \n\n- $f'(x)>0$ on $(-\\infty,2) \\cup (4,+\\infty),$ while\n\n- $f'(x)<0$ on $(2,4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107065 #edb/ref 1065 2661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107065 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a75bc2f2-f4fa-50c8-9eaa-230212f35132" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107065 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
